<section class="hero" style="margin:-34px -20px 30px; padding:34px 20px;">
  <h1>NYC's trash bin mandate is now being enforced.</h1>
  <p class="lede">If your building has 1 to 9 residential units, the black-bag era is over. Trash must go out in an official NYC Bin - and fines started September 8, 2026.</p>
  <div class="alert"><strong>Enforcement is live.</strong> Since Tuesday, September 8, 2026, DSNY issues summonses starting at <strong>$50</strong> for trash set out in anything other than an official NYC Bin. The warning period ended September 7.</div>
</section>

<h2>The rules at a glance</h2>
<div class="fact-grid">
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">Who must comply</div><div class="v">Buildings with 1-9 residential units</div><div class="n">Plus city agencies, nonprofits, houses of worship, and professional offices in residential buildings that get DSNY collection. Roughly 750,000 buildings citywide.</div></div>
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">What's required</div><div class="v">Official NYC Bin for trash</div><div class="n">Any other bin - even a good one with a lid - no longer counts for trash setout. Recycling and compost bins are still your choice.</div></div>
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">Set-out time</div><div class="v">After 6:00 PM</div><div class="n">Bins go out after 6 PM, and everything must be at the curb by midnight the night before collection.</div></div>
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">Fines</div><div class="v">$50 / $100 / $200</div><div class="n">First, second, and third-plus offenses. Enforcement began September 8, 2026.</div></div>
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">Where to buy</div><div class="v">Home Depot</div><div class="n">Every NYC Home Depot stocks the trash bins. Order for pickup at homedepot.com/NYCBins or delivery via DoorDash, Instacart, or Uber Eats.</div></div>
  <div class="fact"><div class="k">What it costs</div><div class="v">$45-$55 per bin</div><div class="n">25 gal $45.19 &middot; 35 gal $47.25 &middot; 45 gal $54.60 (prices as of May 2026). The city priced these below comparable retail bins.</div></div>
</div>

<h2>The short version</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Trash:</strong> official NYC Bin only. No bags, no other cans.</li>
  <li><strong>Compost:</strong> any sealed, clearly labeled bin of 55 gallons or less. The official NYC compost bin is optional.</li>
  <li><strong>Recycling:</strong> any labeled bin, or a clear plastic bag (blue bin optional).</li>
  <li><strong>Owners, not renters:</strong> the property owner or building manager is responsible for buying the bins - and for the fines.</li>
  <li><strong>10+ units:</strong> bigger buildings are on a separate track (stationary on-street Empire Bins). See the <a href="timeline.html">timeline</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which bin do you need?</h2>
<table class="rules">
  <tr><th>Size</th><th>Price</th><th>Holds</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
  <tr><td>45 gallon</td><td>$54.60</td><td>Up to 4 kitchen trash bags</td><td>Properties with multiple units</td></tr>
  <tr><td>35 gallon</td><td>$47.25</td><td>Up to 3 kitchen trash bags</td><td>Small single-family homes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>25 gallon</td><td>$45.19</td><td>Up to 2 kitchen trash bags</td><td>Tight spaces or low trash volume</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="updated">Prices from Home Depot via SILive, May 2026. Compost (13 and 21 gal) and recycling (35 and 45 gal) NYC Bins also exist but are optional. Buildings can put out as many bins as they need - buy enough to hold all your trash.</p>
